+## Current activation (momentum cost-sensitivity on the collected universe — R-001, agent 02)
+
+Closed the decision-relevant qualification left open by the momentum admission
+(37361000967): does the short-horizon momentum edge survive conservative
+transaction-cost stress? Bounded, predeclared cost grid (lookback {3,5,10} x
+cost multiplier {0,1,2,5} of the repository's existing realistic cost model
+`commission_per_trade=2.0, commission_per_share=0.003, slippage_cents=2.0,
+slippage_proportional=0.0005`), each cell judged by the existing walk-forward
+regime-stability gate (train=252d/test=84d/warmup=60d/overlap=60d, 4 contiguous
+volatility blocks, min 400 bars/segment) across all 10 collected tickers
+against a matched coin-flip null charged the same costs. New check
+`research/checks/momentum_cost_sensitivity.py` (artifact
+`state/check_artifacts/momentum_cost_sensitivity_results.json`), independent
+verifier `research/checks/verify_momentum_cost_sensitivity.py` (fresh
+`walk_forward` path, no `stress_segments`), and unit tests
+`tests/test_momentum_cost_sensitivity.py`.
+
+Predeclared cost-robustness classification (per asset, reference lookback 5;
+EDGE = REGIME_STABLE with uniformly positive segment medians; class keys on the
+highest cost multiplier at which an EDGE remains): COST_ROBUST=7 (AAPL, MSFT,
+GOOGL, AMZN, META, TSLA, JPM), COST_MODERATE=0, COST_FRAGILE=0,
+ZERO_COST_ONLY=0, NO_EDGE=3 (NVDA, JNJ, XOM). The 0x lookback-5 column
+reproduces `momentum_results.json` exactly (7 REGIME_STABLE / 1 REGIME_DEPENDENT
+/ 2 CONSISTENT_WITH_NOISE), confirming the harness matches the admitted
+baseline.
+
+Key finding (methodological, decision-relevant): the candidate's per-fold median
+log returns are essentially cost-invariant — 9/10 assets move <= 0.01 from 0x to
+5x (only NVDA, the weak/negative asset, moves 0.106) — because per-fold cost
+drag (~0.15% at 5x) is tiny relative to the ~5-13% per-fold momentum returns.
+However, the matched coin-flip null is NOT turnover-matched to momentum (random
+signs flip far more often than a persistent momentum sign), so charging costs
+degrades the null faster than the candidate: null medians become unstable
+(range up to 0.61; implausible +0.40/+0.50 values for NVDA/TSLA at 1x-2x).
+Consequently the null-relative REGIME_STABLE verdict — and therefore the
+predeclared COST_ROBUST class — is partly null-driven and is NOT a clean
+cost-robustness test. The defensible reading: the momentum edge's MAGNITUDE is
+cost-robust (candidate medians survive 5x realistic costs), but the null-based
+verdict/classification is confounded by turnover mismatch and must be read
+alongside the candidate-absolute diagnostics now stored in the artifact.
+
+Verification (executed): `python3 research/checks/momentum_cost_sensitivity.py`
+ran end-to-end (manifest 10/10 OK, preflight PASSED, leakage PASS on AMZN/JPM,
+determinism r1==r2 True); `python3
+research/checks/verify_momentum_cost_sensitivity.py` all four gates MATCH (fresh
+walk_forward + noise_benchmark recomputation for AMZN/JPM/MSFT/NVDA across all
+lookbacks x costs; 0x lookback-5 cross-check vs momentum_results.json;
+classification reproducibility; determinism); `python3 -m unittest discover -s
+tests` = 181 tests OK (168 baseline + 13 new).
+
+Frontier update: momentum cost-magnitude robustness is VERIFIED; the
+null-turnover confound is a new, explicitly recorded methodological gap. Next
+candidate: implement a turnover-matched null and re-run the cost grid so the
+cost verdict is not confounded (candidate task C-001). This does not overturn
+the momentum admission; it qualifies it (magnitude cost-robust; null-relative
+cost verdict pending a turnover-matched benchmark).
+
